---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Topbar from '../components/Topbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import CopyCommand from '../components/CopyCommand.astro';
import SubscribeForm from '../components/SubscribeForm.astro';
import { SITE, BENCHMARK_FIELDS, HERO_CMD, CLOSER_CMD, BENCH_CMD } from '../config';

const description =
  'One command reads your own GitHub history and prints what code review, rework and delay ' +
  'are costing you — against the time AI saved you writing the code. Runs locally, sends nothing.';
---

<Base title="verification-tax" description={description}>
  <Topbar />

  <main id="main">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>You know what Copilot costs. You don&rsquo;t know what it costs to <em>check its work.</em></h1>
      <p class="sub">
        One command reads your own GitHub history and prints the second number &mdash; review hours,
        rework, delay &mdash; against the time AI saved you writing the code. <b
          >Your machine. Your token. Nothing sent anywhere.</b
        >
      </p>

      <CopyCommand cmd={HERO_CMD} label="hero" />
      <p class="cmd-note">
        No account. No OAuth app. No data leaves your machine. <b
          >MIT licensed &mdash; <a href={SITE.repo}>read the source</a> before you run it.</b
        >
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="output" style="padding-top: 12px;">
      <div class="term" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Sample output of the verification-tax command"><pre><span class="d">  Reading 6,412 pull requests from acme (18 repos)...          [done]
  Nothing sent anywhere. Report written to ./tax.html</span>

<span class="h">  THE VERIFICATION TAX &mdash; acme, Sep 2025 &ndash; Sep 2026</span>

  Engineers                            142
  PRs merged                         6,412      <span class="d">+61% YoY</span>
  Median time to first review       14h 20m     <span class="d">78th pctile, n=10</span>
  Median time in review             31h 05m     <span class="d">71st pctile, n=10</span>
  Merged with no human review          18.4%    <span class="d">1,180 PRs</span>
  Rework rate (churn under 21d)        29.7%    <span class="r">412 later reverted</span>
  Review load, top decile            11.4 h/wk  <span class="r">14 people carry 41%</span>

<span class="h">  DEBITS</span>

  Review hours consumed            18,940 h/yr  <span class="d">&euro;1,477,000</span>
  Rework on already-merged code     6,210 h/yr  <span class="d">&euro;  484,000</span>
  Cost of delay, PRs over 5 days         &mdash;      <span class="d">&euro;  212,000</span>
  AI tooling spend                       &mdash;      <span class="d">&euro;  204,000</span>
                                              <span class="d">&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;</span>
                                               <span class="r">&euro;2,377,000</span>

<span class="h">  CREDITS</span>

  Authoring hours saved            21,300 h/yr  <span class="g">&euro;1,661,000</span>
                                              <span class="d">&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;&mdash;</span>
<span class="h">  NET</span>                                          <span class="r">&minus;&euro;716,000</span>

<span class="d">  Sensitivity: &minus;&euro;1.2M to +&euro;310k across the plausible range.
  Every coefficient lives in ./tax.config.json. Disagree, edit, rerun.
  Slide for the board meeting: ./tax-board-slide.png</span></pre></div>
      <p class="term-cap">
        Sample output. Your numbers will not look like this &mdash; that is rather the point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="why">
      <h2>Three reasons this is worth one command</h2>
      <div class="pillars">
        <div class="pillar">
          <span class="k" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <h3>It is your org&rsquo;s number, not a survey of somebody else&rsquo;s</h3>
          <p>
            It reads your merged pull requests &mdash; how long they waited, how long they took, how
            much got rewritten inside three weeks, how many shipped with no human ever looking
            &mdash; and turns that into a figure with <b>your company&rsquo;s name on it</b>.
          </p>
          <p>Every benchmark report you have read this year was about other people&rsquo;s teams.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pillar">
          <span class="k" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <h3>Nothing leaves your laptop, so nobody has to approve it</h3>
          <p>
            No account, no OAuth app, no vendor questionnaire, no DPA, no six-week security review
            attached to a free thing. It uses the GitHub token already in your shell and writes an
            HTML file next to it.
          </p>
          <p><b>You can run this before lunch without asking anyone.</b></p>
        </div>
        <div class="pillar">
          <span class="k" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <h3>It comes out as a slide, not another dashboard</h3>
          <p>
            One page for the meeting where somebody asks what the AI spend actually bought. Every
            coefficient is sourced to DORA, METR, Faros and GitClear &mdash; and sits in a config
            file <b>you can edit the moment you disagree</b>.
          </p>
          <p>
            You are going to be asked this question. Better to have run the numbers yourself first.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="distrust">
      <div class="distrust">
        <h2>Reasons to distrust this number</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every tool in this category opens with why you should believe it. Here is the other list,
          because you were going to work it out anyway and it is faster if we start there.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>It is an estimate, not a stopwatch.</b> Review hours are inferred from pull request size,
            reviewer count and elapsed time. Nobody is timing anyone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The coefficients are other people&rsquo;s research.</b> They are sourced and dated, and
            yours will differ. That is why they are in a text file rather than compiled in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>It cannot see the review that happened at a desk</b>, in a Slack thread, or in
            someone&rsquo;s head on the walk home. It will undercount real diligence in teams that talk
            more than they comment.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>It will flatter or damn you for reasons that predate AI.</b> A slow review culture in 2023
            is still a slow review culture now. Run it against a pre-2024 window first and read the delta,
            not the absolute.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>It measures cost, not value.</b> A negative net is not automatically an argument to spend
            less. It is an argument to find out where the money is going.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The peer set is self-selected and small.</b> It is made of orgs that already suspected they
            had a review problem and went looking. That is not the industry, and percentiles only appear
            once enough orgs have opted in for them to mean anything.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The model can produce a positive net</b>, and for some orgs it does. If yours is negative
            across the entire sensitivity range, that is a finding rather than a default.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If any of that changes the answer materially, <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}
            >open an issue</a
          >. The model is a single JSON file you can read in a minute, and being argued with in
          public is the fastest way to make it right.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="fit">
      <h2 class="small">Whether this is for you</h2>
      <div class="fit">
        <div>
          <h3>Run it if</h3>
          <ul class="yes">
            <li>You have ten or more engineers and at least a year of GitHub history</li>
            <li>Someone above you has started asking what the AI tooling line item is returning</li>
            <li>
              Your senior engineers have said the word &ldquo;drowning&rdquo; out loud in the last
              quarter
            </li>
            <li>You want a defensible figure before the budget conversation, not during it</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Don&rsquo;t bother if</h3>
          <ul class="no">
            <li>You are a handful of people &mdash; below about ten engineers the numbers are noise</li>
            <li>You are on GitLab or Bitbucket &mdash; not yet, and the issue tracker is where to say so</li>
            <li>You want a live dashboard. This is a command that prints a page and exits.</li>
            <li>You need the number to come out a particular way</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="benchmark">
      <div class="bench">
        <h2 class="small">Optional: find out where you sit</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The percentiles in the output come from orgs that chose to send theirs. If you want yours
          included, and want to see where you land against everyone else who opted in:
        </p>
        <div class="flag">$ {BENCH_CMD}</div>
        <p class="body">
          That flag sends twelve integers and nothing else. No code, no file names, no repository
          names, no company name, no email &mdash; unless you pass <code>--contact</code>. Here is the
          entire payload:
        </p>
        <ul class="sends">
          {BENCHMARK_FIELDS.map(([name]) => <li>{name}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="body" style="max-width:62ch;">
          When enough orgs have opted in, the aggregate publishes in full, free, with the dataset
          attached &mdash; the first cross-org measurement of verification cost that isn&rsquo;t owned
          by somebody selling into the category. If you want that when it lands:
        </p>
        <SubscribeForm />
        <p class="mail-note">
          One email, when the report is out. No sequence, no drip, no newsletter. Unsubscribe is a
          link, not a conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closer">
      <h2>Run it on your own org before somebody runs it on you.</h2>
      <CopyCommand cmd={CLOSER_CMD} label="closer" style="margin-top:24px;" />
      <p class="cmd-note">
        Minutes, not hours &mdash; and it caches, so the second run is instant. Nothing leaves your
        machine. <a href={SITE.repo}>Source on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer full />
</Base>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var live = document.createElement('p');
    live.className = 'visually-hidden';
    live.setAttribute('role', 'status');
    document.body.appendChild(live);

    function flash(btn, text) {
      var original = btn.textContent;
      btn.textContent = 'Copied';
      btn.classList.add('done');
      live.textContent = text + ' copied to clipboard';
      setTimeout(function () {
        btn.textContent = original;
        btn.classList.remove('done');
        live.textContent = '';
      }, 1800);
    }

    Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('button.copy'), function (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var text = btn.getAttribute('data-copy');

        function done() {
          flash(btn, 'Command');
          if (window.plausible) {
            window.plausible('copy_command', { props: { which: btn.getAttribute('data-label') } });
          }
        }

        function fallback() {
          var ta = document.createElement('textarea');
          ta.value = text;
          ta.setAttribute('readonly', '');
          ta.style.position = 'absolute';
          ta.style.left = '-9999px';
          document.body.appendChild(ta);
          ta.select();
          try {
            document.execCommand('copy');
            done();
          } catch (e) {
            btn.textContent = 'Press ⌘C';
            live.textContent = 'Copy failed. Select the command and copy it manually.';
          }
          document.body.removeChild(ta);
        }

        if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
          navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).then(done, fallback);
        } else {
          fallback();
        }
      });
    });
  })();
</script>
